From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luke Craig <lacraig3@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 23:33:21 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] target/i386: key an int 0x80 syscall on the whole stack
 pointer

x86_sc_i386() kept the user stack pointer in a uint32_t and gave it to
the syscall trap as the task key, but the return (x86_sc_return) looks
the call up with the whole rsp.  For an int 0x80 from a 64-bit task the
two never matched: every such call lost its return, and its pending
entry was dropped as stale when the next call came (found by
tests/syscall_cover.py and tests/syscall_truth.py).  Key int 0x80 on the
whole register.  The vDSO's fast paths (sysenter, and syscall from a
32-bit task) keep the 32-bit value: the kernel returns them with a
zero-extended one.

SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-or-later
Origin: new
---
 target/i386/tcg/seg_helper.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/target/i386/tcg/seg_helper.c b/target/i386/tcg/seg_helper.c
index 007a7122273df7296a87b1ed6b9d3b8f1e6b3a13..e75bd32d9c7677e6898b369b1b49e664fe2ab4c4 100644
--- a/target/i386/tcg/seg_helper.c
+++ b/target/i386/tcg/seg_helper.c
@@ -1257,6 +1257,12 @@ bool x86_sc_i386(CPUX86State *env, target_ulong next, unsigned len,
     CPUState *cs = env_cpu(env);
     uint64_t args[6], ret, pc = env->segs[R_CS].base + next;
     uint32_t sp = env->regs[usp], arg6 = 0;
+    /*
+     * The task key: the stack pointer as the call returns with it.  An
+     * int 0x80 returns with the whole register (a 64-bit task's rsp), the
+     * vDSO's fast paths with a 32-bit one.
+     */
+    uint64_t usp_key = fast ? sp : env->regs[usp];
     int r;
 
     for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
@@ -1281,7 +1287,7 @@ bool x86_sc_i386(CPUX86State *env, target_ulong next, unsigned len,
     cs->syscall_inject_abis = 1u << SYSCALL_ABI_X86_64;   /* not an i386 task's */
     while ((r = syscall_trap_enter(cs, SYSCALL_ABI_I386,
                                    (uint32_t)env->regs[R_EAX], args, pc, len,
-                                   x86_sc_key(env), sp, x86_sc_percpu(env),
+                                   x86_sc_key(env), usp_key, x86_sc_percpu(env),
                                    X86_SC_KMMU, &ret)) & SYSCALL_TRAP_FAULT) {
         syscall_trap_fault(cs, 0);
     }
